# What `docker build` is allowed to send to the daemon.
#
# An allowlist, not a denylist, and it mirrors the `COPY` lines in `Containerfile` one for
# one. A denylist was the obvious first shape and it is the wrong one here: the context is
# the whole working tree, so every directory anybody adds later -- a second build tree, a
# cache, a downloaded SDK -- is included until somebody remembers to add a line. The cost of
# forgetting is not a bad image (the `COPY` lines decide that) but a build that hands the
# daemon the entire tree before it starts.
#
# The number that made this worth writing: 3.8 GB, measured on a development Mac, of which
# `src/desktop/src-tauri/target` alone was 3.6 GB. The same build on a server that had never
# run `make tauri-bundle` was 66 MB, so this is invisible exactly where it is cheap and
# expensive exactly where people work. `make container-image TARGET=you@box` pays it twice,
# once to pack and once to stream.
#
# The rule when `Containerfile` learns a new `COPY`: add the matching `!` line here. A
# missing one fails the build loudly (`COPY failed: no source files`), which is the failure
# worth having.
*

!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!LICENSE
!src

# `src/desktop` is the Tauri shell: not a Python package, excluded from the wheel by
# `[tool.setuptools.packages.find] exclude = ["desktop*"]`, and never installed by the
# `pip install .` this image runs. It is re-excluded after `!src` rather than being left out
# of the allowlist because `src` has to come in whole for `mcp_gateway` and `mcp_gateway_ui`.
# This single line is the 3.6 GB.
src/desktop
